<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikolai Bobrikov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikolai Bobrikov was a Russian general who served as Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from 1898. He drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> of the grand duchy, imposing the February Manifesto of 1899, which allowed imperial laws to override Finnish ones, the language manifesto of 1900 and a conscription law of 1901. He was shot in Helsinki in June 1904 by the Finnish official Eugen Schauman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikolai-bobrikov.jpg" alt="Nikolai Bobrikov" width="261" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikolai_Bobrikov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikolay Ivanovich Bobrikov was born in the village of Strelna near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. His father, Ivan Bobrikov, was a military physician and his mother, Alexandra Seeland, was a Baltic German. He attended the 1st Cadet Corps and became an officer in 1858, later taking a degree at the Nikolay general staff academy.</p><p>He served on the staff of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> military district and from 1867 as chief of staff of the 22nd infantry division in Novgorod, and became a colonel in 1869. In 1876 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> as Russian military official, and in 1878 he became a major general during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>. He was promoted to lieutenant general in 1884, becoming chief of staff of the guard and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> military district, and to general of the infantry in 1897. He was an energetic and capable man whose views were strongly Russian nationalist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> appointed Bobrikov Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and of the Finnish Military District on 29 August 1898. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> programme aimed, among other things, to unify the Finnish army, make Russian the language of the Senate, education and administration, bring the University of Helsinki under tighter control, let Russian citizens hold office in Finland, end the Finnish markka and remove the customs border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>He was involved in creating the February Manifesto of 1899, which began what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> remember as the first Years of Oppression and allowed the Diet to be overruled when imperial interest required. Half a million Finns signed a petition asking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> to revoke it, but the Emperor did not even receive the delegation. In 1900 Bobrikov ordered that correspondence between government offices be conducted in Russian. The Finnish army was abolished in 1901, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> could be made to serve with Russian troops anywhere in the empire; only 42% of the conscripts reported to the first call-up in 1902. In 1903 the Emperor gave him dictatorial powers to dismiss officials and close newspapers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bobrikov became very unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> as an adamant supporter of curtailing the grand duchy's extensive autonomy. On 16 June 1904 Eugen Schauman, a Finnish nationalist born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a>, shot him three times in Helsinki and then shot himself twice. Schauman died instantly, while Bobrikov, mortally wounded, died in hospital in the early hours of the following morning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikolay Bobrikov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1899 Russification threatened Finnish autonomy, and Governor-General <b>Bobrikov</b> was assassinated in 1904.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Grand Duchy of Finland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Strelna, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Helsinki, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.85,30.06&amp;year=1881" aria-label="Strelna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5180 157 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikolai Bobrikov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5630 427h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5630 427h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.85,30.06&amp;year=1881">What was here in 1881? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
